00:03:28.642 --> 00:03:31.692
And I was just a face in
the crowd but let's go

00:03:31.692 --> 00:03:33.882
into what you now do.

00:03:33.882 --> 00:03:36.342
So for anyone who's been living

00:03:36.342 --> 00:03:40.355
under a rock for the past
10 or so years, what is edX?

00:03:43.352 --> 00:03:48.352
- EdX is a learning platform
where we connect some

00:03:50.652 --> 00:03:52.132
of the teachers and professors

00:03:52.132 --> 00:03:56.462
from the greatest institutions
in the world like MIT

00:03:56.462 --> 00:04:00.332
and Harvard, Stanford,
Berkeley, Oxford and others

00:04:00.332 --> 00:04:01.702
to learners from all over the world.

00:04:01.702 --> 00:04:04.619
So it's a platform where the
learners who want to learn

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:07.872
can come in and learn from
some of the best professors

00:04:07.872 --> 00:04:10.122
and teachers around the world.

00:04:10.122 --> 00:04:13.292
And also from companies like
Amazon web services, Google,

00:04:13.292 --> 00:04:15.022
IBM and others.

00:04:15.022 --> 00:04:19.392
We have about 40 million
learners on our platform

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:22.612
where learners come in and
they can learn anything

00:04:22.612 --> 00:04:24.482
from the humanities to computer science

00:04:24.482 --> 00:04:27.872
to data science, business, arts.

00:04:27.872 --> 00:04:31.372
You could even learn how to
use mechanical ventilators.

00:04:31.372 --> 00:04:34.102
The course that we launched
with Harvard and (indistinct)

00:04:34.102 --> 00:04:37.102
during COVID times about a year ago.

00:04:37.102 --> 00:04:39.258
And people can also own credentials.

00:04:39.258 --> 00:04:43.772
We have these modular
credentials like micro masters

00:04:43.772 --> 00:04:47.682
and micro bachelor's that
people can use to up-skill

00:04:47.682 --> 00:04:49.062
in their current job.

00:04:49.062 --> 00:04:50.590
Or if you've been
furlough, you can use that

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.472
to gain new skills for the future.

00:04:53.472 --> 00:04:55.472
So this is who edX is and you can come in

00:04:55.472 --> 00:04:57.852
and you can start learning.

00:04:57.852 --> 00:04:59.802
You can learn for free on the platform.

00:05:00.782 --> 00:05:05.282
- How did all of this come to be?

00:05:05.282 --> 00:05:08.362
You were a professor, a very
successful professor at MIT.

00:05:08.362 --> 00:05:12.472
You had your tenure,
you're running departments.

00:05:12.472 --> 00:05:15.682
You kind of gave that up to do this.

00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:18.262
What was the evolution of going

00:05:18.262 --> 00:05:21.185
from professor to edX and
making this your life?

00:05:22.487 --> 00:05:24.437
- Sal, you had something to do with it.

00:05:26.322 --> 00:05:29.382
I would trace it back
to, so for me education

00:05:33.002 --> 00:05:35.192
has always been something
near and dear to my heart.

00:05:35.192 --> 00:05:38.542
I remember as a physics
student in IT Madras

00:05:38.542 --> 00:05:40.389
in my first year, I
was one of two students

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:43.222
out of 300 that failed
the first physics exam

00:05:43.222 --> 00:05:45.682
and my heart completely stopped.

00:05:45.682 --> 00:05:48.172
And at that time I realized that everybody

00:05:48.172 --> 00:05:50.992
around me seemed to understand
calculus based physics.

00:05:50.992 --> 00:05:53.152
There was no Khan Academy then remember.

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:54.402
And I was here.

00:05:54.402 --> 00:05:56.272
I hadn't learned calculus based physics

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:58.772
and I had no idea what
they were talking about.

00:05:58.772 --> 00:06:00.902
And so I said, "Look, hopefully

00:06:00.902 --> 00:06:03.912
there'll be one day when
everybody will have equal access

00:06:03.912 --> 00:06:06.849
to education and come up to
speed as everybody else."

00:06:07.692 --> 00:06:10.232
I really got a chance in the year 2000

00:06:10.232 --> 00:06:13.162
when MIT launched
OpenCourseWare, I was one

00:06:13.162 --> 00:06:14.602
of the early professors to launch

00:06:14.602 --> 00:06:17.162
my circuits course on OpenCourseWare.

00:06:17.162 --> 00:06:19.112
And then, Sal I've heard you talk about

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:20.629
how you were inspired by OpenCourseWare

00:06:20.629 --> 00:06:22.462
and you launched a Khan Academy.

00:06:22.462 --> 00:06:27.222
And I heard about your videos
from my children and others.

00:06:27.222 --> 00:06:30.945
And at MIT, a number of
us were thinking about

00:06:30.945 --> 00:06:33.512
what should the future look like?

00:06:33.512 --> 00:06:34.962
We did OpenCourseWare.

00:06:34.962 --> 00:06:37.472
What would the next big thing look like?

00:06:37.472 --> 00:06:38.832
And then there's a confluence

00:06:38.832 --> 00:06:42.002
of multiple technologies
happened around 2008, 2009

00:06:42.002 --> 00:06:45.782
like social networking,
video distribution at scale,

00:06:45.782 --> 00:06:48.952
cloud computing, mobile computing,

00:06:48.952 --> 00:06:52.392
at that time it was very clear that

00:06:52.392 --> 00:06:54.412
we could do learning at scale.

00:06:54.412 --> 00:06:56.912
And I launched a program called WebSim

00:06:56.912 --> 00:07:00.212
as part of our lab where I said, "If

00:07:00.212 --> 00:07:03.652
I could teach people how to
do circuits labs fully online

00:07:03.652 --> 00:07:05.852
then maybe this is something
we could do at scale."

00:07:05.852 --> 00:07:08.872
And so on the average day,
two to 300 students from all

00:07:08.872 --> 00:07:12.512
over the world would come
in and do WebSim which is

00:07:12.512 --> 00:07:13.792
I think of it as a MOOC lab.

00:07:13.792 --> 00:07:17.069
And this was, I built it out in 2003.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:19.242
- MOOC being massively open online course.

00:07:20.549 --> 00:07:23.182
Before (indistinct) open
online courses existed.

00:07:23.182 --> 00:07:26.412
- This was nine years
before they existed, 2003.

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:28.242
You could maybe call it a MOOL,

00:07:28.242 --> 00:07:30.252
massive open online labs.

00:07:30.252 --> 00:07:31.562
It was circuits labs.

00:07:31.562 --> 00:07:34.822
And I figured if I could
do online circuits labs

00:07:34.822 --> 00:07:37.742
then maybe I could do other things.

00:07:37.742 --> 00:07:39.015
But then I did nothing beyond that.

00:07:39.015 --> 00:07:41.172
I just kept running the WebSim

00:07:41.172 --> 00:07:45.242
and then inspired by you Sal in 2011,

00:07:45.242 --> 00:07:47.802
a number of us at MIT got together.

00:07:47.802 --> 00:07:50.222
The leadership at MIT,
Raphael Reif, myself,

00:07:50.222 --> 00:07:52.342
leadership Harvard,
Alan Garber and others.

00:07:52.342 --> 00:07:54.682
We've been thinking about
how could we take this

00:07:54.682 --> 00:07:55.762
to the next level?

00:07:55.762 --> 00:07:57.112
And that's how edX was born.

00:07:57.112 --> 00:07:58.592
When we said, "Look, rather

00:07:58.592 --> 00:08:01.172
than just having course materials
as an open course, where

00:08:01.172 --> 00:08:04.652
what if we take complete
courses with certificates

00:08:04.652 --> 00:08:07.532
with communities and also with labs

00:08:07.532 --> 00:08:09.342
and real hands-on experiences

00:08:09.342 --> 00:08:11.612
and make that available to the world.

00:08:11.612 --> 00:08:15.962
And that's when we launched
MITx in December of 2011

00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:18.312
and turned into edX when we partnered

00:08:18.312 --> 00:08:22.232
with Harvard and MIT in
early May and launched edX,

00:08:22.232 --> 00:08:24.622
that's how it all came together.

00:08:24.622 --> 00:08:29.382
- And what made you decide to
make this your life journey?

00:08:29.382 --> 00:08:31.922
As I said, you had a
good gig as a professor,

00:08:31.922 --> 00:08:33.095
head of departments.

00:08:35.852 --> 00:08:37.623
- You know what, it's
one of those things where

00:08:37.623 --> 00:08:42.492
as far as life is concerned,

00:08:42.492 --> 00:08:45.932
I'm very much an instrumentalist.

00:08:45.932 --> 00:08:47.902
I don't plan very far out.

00:08:47.902 --> 00:08:51.322
And I sort of take it one base at a time

00:08:51.322 --> 00:08:53.725
to use a baseball analogy.

00:08:54.672 --> 00:08:58.152
And so when the opportunity arose

00:08:59.072 --> 00:09:02.402
we decided we'd create a MOOC platform.

00:09:02.402 --> 00:09:05.005
And they initially called
MITx and then called edX.

00:09:06.462 --> 00:09:09.302
I was the head of the computer
science and AI lab at MIT.

00:09:09.302 --> 00:09:12.642
And it had a 25 strong research group

00:09:12.642 --> 00:09:15.832
with PhD students and postdocs
and undergrads and so on.

00:09:15.832 --> 00:09:19.782
And so we incubated what would
become edX in the basement

00:09:19.782 --> 00:09:23.182
of CSAIL, the computer
science and AI lab at MIT.

00:09:23.182 --> 00:09:26.512
And I was looking to
recruit professors to teach

00:09:26.512 --> 00:09:28.552
the first course on edX.

00:09:28.552 --> 00:09:29.972
And when I told people, yeah

00:09:29.972 --> 00:09:32.042
the course needs to start in two months

00:09:32.042 --> 00:09:33.182
and oh, by the way we are building

00:09:33.182 --> 00:09:34.302
a completely new platform.

00:09:34.302 --> 00:09:36.937
We wanted an open source
platform that uses Python

00:09:36.937 --> 00:09:39.262
and Django and the latest technologies

00:09:39.262 --> 00:09:41.442
and not the old stuff, cloud-based,

00:09:41.442 --> 00:09:43.552
there's nothing, nothing
available at that time.

00:09:43.552 --> 00:09:46.662
And so my colleague said,
"You're nuts, can't do it."

00:09:46.662 --> 00:09:49.629
And so, by that time we had announced edX.

00:09:50.494 --> 00:09:52.122
There was no one ready to teach something.

00:09:52.122 --> 00:09:53.852
And so, we announced it

00:09:53.852 --> 00:09:55.146
and so I jumped in and said,

00:09:55.146 --> 00:09:57.917
"Look, we can launch
with the circuit course."

00:09:57.917 --> 00:10:01.372
And people looked to me as if I was crazy.

00:10:01.372 --> 00:10:03.862
Why would you launch with a
circuits course as opposed

00:10:03.862 --> 00:10:06.542
to something like machine
learning or Python programming?

00:10:06.542 --> 00:10:08.518
I said, "Look, that's the best we can do.

00:10:08.518 --> 00:10:11.632
I could not get my colleagues
to do this quickly enough.

00:10:11.632 --> 00:10:12.802
They felt we were nuts."

00:10:12.802 --> 00:10:15.746
And so that's how we launched
with the circuits course.

00:10:15.746 --> 00:10:17.702
And I kinda got sucked into it having

00:10:17.702 --> 00:10:21.512
to do the first course
myself, with several

00:10:21.512 --> 00:10:25.002
of my colleagues like
Jerry Sussman, Jacob White,

00:10:25.002 --> 00:10:27.622
Chris Terman and others
who were really jumped in

00:10:27.622 --> 00:10:29.272
and pitched in to make it happen.

00:10:30.709 --> 00:10:32.822
- It's a great story.

00:10:32.822 --> 00:10:36.242
And I do wanna ask
where this is all going,

00:10:36.242 --> 00:10:37.432
where do you see the future?

00:10:37.432 --> 00:10:39.372
But I'm getting an unusual number

00:10:39.372 --> 00:10:41.202
of questions coming in
through the message board.

00:10:41.202 --> 00:10:44.842
So I wanna make sure I
get to some of these.

00:10:44.842 --> 00:10:46.452
This is just kind of a fun question

00:10:46.452 --> 00:10:49.602
from YouTube, Anthonine Apolius.

00:10:49.602 --> 00:10:51.192
What's your favorite thing in math?

00:10:51.192 --> 00:10:54.312
And maybe I'll expand Anthonine's
question a little bit.

00:10:54.312 --> 00:10:55.145
What's your favorite thing

00:10:55.145 --> 00:10:57.145
in all of knowledge, in all of learning?

00:10:59.496 --> 00:11:01.172
- I'll answer both questions.

00:11:01.172 --> 00:11:03.132
My favorite thing in math actually was

00:11:04.272 --> 00:11:06.852
when I really one day
I kind of just got it.

00:11:06.852 --> 00:11:09.982
I really figured out how to
solve differential equations

00:11:09.982 --> 00:11:13.092
which is that we try not to overthink it.

00:11:13.092 --> 00:11:14.872
You just kind of do it mechanically

00:11:14.872 --> 00:11:17.222
and you apply templates and do it.

00:11:17.222 --> 00:11:20.052
And actually I remember
in my circuits course

00:11:20.052 --> 00:11:22.202
we use first and second
order differential equations

00:11:22.202 --> 00:11:23.892
and solutions of those.

00:11:23.892 --> 00:11:27.392
And I actually called
up my student who had

00:11:27.392 --> 00:11:30.542
a little gig going in California.

00:11:30.542 --> 00:11:32.052
And I called him up and said, "Hey look,

00:11:32.052 --> 00:11:35.822
I need, your website doesn't
have a video tutorial

00:11:35.822 --> 00:11:38.892
on first order differential equations.

00:11:38.892 --> 00:11:40.822
So I need you to create something for me."

00:11:40.822 --> 00:11:41.662
And this guy did it.

00:11:41.662 --> 00:11:43.142
And his name was Khan Academy.

00:11:43.142 --> 00:11:45.112
So in my course on edX, I have a tutorial

00:11:45.112 --> 00:11:48.149
from Sal Khan on some of the stuff.

00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:53.072
But in terms of, in all of learning

00:11:53.072 --> 00:11:54.085
I think the thing,

00:11:55.682 --> 00:11:59.572
my dream is one day to really
understand quantum mechanics.

00:11:59.572 --> 00:12:01.352
I don't understand it.

00:12:01.352 --> 00:12:02.912
I've never really gotten it deeply

00:12:02.912 --> 00:12:06.132
I can do the math and do
some of the formulate stuff.

00:12:06.132 --> 00:12:09.062
But my dream one day is to
really have the time to sit

00:12:09.062 --> 00:12:10.782
down and listen to five minutes lectures

00:12:10.782 --> 00:12:13.482
and others and really, really,
truly, (indistinct) it.

00:12:14.386 --> 00:12:15.725
I'm not there yet.

00:12:17.232 --> 00:12:21.192
- It's interesting, my
quantum mechanics and sorry,

00:12:21.192 --> 00:12:22.742
are you getting a little
buzz every time I talk,

00:12:22.742 --> 00:12:23.602
every time I start to talk

00:12:23.602 --> 00:12:25.919
it sounds like there's a
little, did you hear that?

00:12:25.919 --> 00:12:26.792
- No, no I'm okay.

00:12:26.792 --> 00:12:28.262
- Oh, good, good, good, good.

00:12:28.262 --> 00:12:29.932
No, when you mention quantum mechanics,

00:12:29.932 --> 00:12:33.702
that is one of those
things that I also want,

00:12:33.702 --> 00:12:36.132
I seek to have a deeper
understanding of it

00:12:36.132 --> 00:12:38.252
but I think anyone, the
first time you learn it

00:12:38.252 --> 00:12:42.322
and you say, "Wow, all
matter has both particle

00:12:42.322 --> 00:12:45.982
and wave-like properties
that the electron is a wave

00:12:45.982 --> 00:12:47.532
until you attempt to observe it."

00:12:47.532 --> 00:12:50.262
And then it collapses, the wave
function collapses and then

00:12:50.262 --> 00:12:51.542
you know its position.

00:12:51.542 --> 00:12:53.672
Like it's so counterintuitive

00:12:53.672 --> 00:12:56.572
to what we think is our understanding

00:12:56.572 --> 00:12:58.112
or our perception of the universe.

00:12:58.112 --> 00:13:01.702
And actually I probably had
the most interesting insight

00:13:01.702 --> 00:13:03.752
that affects my my understanding
of quantum mechanics

00:13:03.752 --> 00:13:07.342
when I was reading this
question and answer.

00:13:07.342 --> 00:13:11.662
This is recently, this is
what an Indian guru who died

00:13:11.662 --> 00:13:14.662
in the 1980s, his name
is Nisargadatta Maharaj

00:13:14.662 --> 00:13:16.522
and he wrote this actually,
he didn't write this book.

00:13:16.522 --> 00:13:19.162
This book "I am that" is based
on conversations with him.

00:13:19.162 --> 00:13:24.162
And he's apparently, had
reached nirvana or moksha

00:13:24.692 --> 00:13:27.062
or had kind of reached enlightenment

00:13:27.062 --> 00:13:29.152
and people are always
asking him these questions.

00:13:29.152 --> 00:13:31.772
And they keep asking him, well,
what is the cause of this?

00:13:31.772 --> 00:13:33.322
And what is the cause of that?

00:13:33.322 --> 00:13:37.522
And he says, "You are so
obsessed with causality.

00:13:37.522 --> 00:13:41.529
What you call causality is
just repeated correlation."

00:13:42.572 --> 00:13:44.352
And I was like, is he right?

00:13:44.352 --> 00:13:46.532
Is it just repeated correlation?

00:13:46.532 --> 00:13:48.152
And I'm still processing that

00:13:48.152 --> 00:13:51.132
but that actually in
some ways opened my mind

00:13:51.132 --> 00:13:53.532
to things like quantum mechanics

00:13:53.532 --> 00:13:58.412
which seem to defy our standard,
our standard intuitions

00:13:58.412 --> 00:14:02.592
whatever the intuition is of
causality and correlation.

00:14:02.592 --> 00:14:03.705
But yeah, I think that's really-

00:14:03.705 --> 00:14:05.468
- I mean absolutely.

00:14:05.468 --> 00:14:08.725
I did have a really,
really deep understanding

00:14:08.725 --> 00:14:11.992
of Newtonian mechanics and so on.

00:14:11.992 --> 00:14:13.355
And maybe that was part of the problem.

00:14:13.355 --> 00:14:15.182
I really deeply got it.

00:14:15.182 --> 00:14:17.792
And then when you have to
completely rethink things

00:14:17.792 --> 00:14:21.082
and change your frame of
reference, almost became

00:14:21.082 --> 00:14:23.489
like one of the Yogi Berra quotes,

00:14:23.489 --> 00:14:24.642
"wherever you go there you are".

00:14:24.642 --> 00:14:26.602
You said, "Yeah, I understand that."

00:14:26.602 --> 00:14:27.932
But when you really think about it deeply,

00:14:27.932 --> 00:14:30.082
you said, "No, I don't got that."

00:14:30.082 --> 00:14:31.542
No idea what that means.

00:14:31.542 --> 00:14:35.742
And the deeper I began thinking
about quantum mechanics

00:14:35.742 --> 00:14:37.592
and things like that, the more I realize

00:14:37.592 --> 00:14:38.662
that while I could do the math

00:14:38.662 --> 00:14:41.801
and do calculations, the speed
of light and the compression

00:14:41.801 --> 00:14:45.122
of time and all of that stuff
and space, I could do the math

00:14:45.122 --> 00:14:47.632
but I never really, really
internalized and got it.

00:14:47.632 --> 00:14:50.032
So my dream one day is
to really, really get it.

00:14:51.232 --> 00:14:54.432
- Yeah, completely agree.

00:14:54.432 --> 00:14:57.432
And it's, yeah, it's, I mean

00:14:57.432 --> 00:14:59.422
I could go on into this but like

00:14:59.422 --> 00:15:01.572
how much is based on perception.

00:15:01.572 --> 00:15:06.152
Anyway, we could go more into this.

00:15:06.152 --> 00:15:10.732
Another fun quote from that
guru was when someone, he's like

00:15:10.732 --> 00:15:13.349
you ascribe reality to certain things

00:15:13.349 --> 00:15:16.242
and you ascribe imagination or dreamlike

00:15:16.242 --> 00:15:18.232
to other things but imagine
if your dreams picked

00:15:18.232 --> 00:15:20.212
off where they left off the night before,

00:15:20.212 --> 00:15:22.962
then all of a sudden you would
have two realities anyway,

00:15:22.962 --> 00:15:25.632
we're going way off topic.

00:15:25.632 --> 00:15:28.032
But quantum mechanics
makes me think of that.

00:15:28.032 --> 00:15:31.132
- I think it goes much better with a beer

00:15:31.132 --> 00:15:32.232
in your hand actually.

00:15:33.722 --> 00:15:37.276
- We're not, a lot of kids are
watching Professor Agarwal,

00:15:37.276 --> 00:15:39.142
you're a role model.

00:15:39.142 --> 00:15:40.792
Be careful what you advocate for.

00:15:41.642 --> 00:15:45.442
So more questions and really good ones.

00:15:45.442 --> 00:15:48.632
Several from (indistinct) on YouTube.

00:15:48.632 --> 00:15:50.222
So one of the questions is,

00:15:50.222 --> 00:15:51.822
Dr. Agarwal, India and the third world

00:15:51.822 --> 00:15:53.442
was hit hard by COVID.

00:15:53.442 --> 00:15:55.342
In one of your talks you had
mentioned that there's a plan

00:15:55.342 --> 00:15:57.861
to put the entire medical
education curriculum online.

00:15:57.861 --> 00:15:59.555
How is that progressing?

00:16:01.502 --> 00:16:04.552
- I did give a talk where
we are talking to a number

00:16:04.552 --> 00:16:07.742
of universities where the idea is,

00:16:07.742 --> 00:16:11.422
could we put an entire
medical curriculum online?

00:16:11.422 --> 00:16:13.896
And then the same applies
to computer science,

00:16:13.896 --> 00:16:16.032
data science, business.

00:16:16.032 --> 00:16:19.272
And we've been sort of
going at it one at a time.

00:16:19.272 --> 00:16:22.082
We made huge inroads into
computer science, data science

00:16:22.082 --> 00:16:24.132
and business on edX.

00:16:24.132 --> 00:16:25.192
We have over 3000 courses

00:16:25.192 --> 00:16:28.022
and we're making great
inroads in these areas.

00:16:28.022 --> 00:16:30.112
I would say healthcare is gonna be next

00:16:30.112 --> 00:16:33.252
where we do have a fair
number of courses at this time

00:16:33.252 --> 00:16:38.252
but the idea would be,
imagine if you could learn

00:16:39.422 --> 00:16:44.422
about topics like biochemistry,
biology, epidemiology,

00:16:44.442 --> 00:16:47.897
pathology, a lot of
the theoretical aspects

00:16:47.897 --> 00:16:49.432
for these subjects

00:16:49.432 --> 00:16:52.902
but then you go to college and
you learn and you practice.

00:16:52.902 --> 00:16:57.002
And you work with real
calibers and all of that stuff.

00:16:57.002 --> 00:16:58.682
And you really, really practice

00:16:58.682 --> 00:17:03.342
and look at patients and work
with patients and sick people.

00:17:03.342 --> 00:17:04.175
That would be the way to do it.

00:17:04.175 --> 00:17:05.652
Sort of blended model of learning

00:17:05.652 --> 00:17:09.022
where what you could learn
online, you sort of learn online

00:17:09.022 --> 00:17:12.932
and then you do all the
practicum stuff when you come

00:17:12.932 --> 00:17:16.142
to a physical medical space.

00:17:16.142 --> 00:17:17.072
And so to me

00:17:17.072 --> 00:17:19.142
that would be something
that is very exciting

00:17:19.142 --> 00:17:21.312
and something that we would be interested

00:17:21.312 --> 00:17:22.592
in doing in the future.

00:17:22.592 --> 00:17:24.972
But again, we started
with computer science,

00:17:24.972 --> 00:17:27.692
data science and business,
where we've really built

00:17:27.692 --> 00:17:29.935
out a pretty deep
curricula in those areas.

00:17:31.382 --> 00:17:35.442
- Yeah, it seems you and
I have definitely talked

00:17:35.442 --> 00:17:36.452
about this in the past

00:17:36.452 --> 00:17:38.392
that the potential for I mean, medicine

00:17:38.392 --> 00:17:40.142
is a big one considering the shortage

00:17:40.142 --> 00:17:42.032
of healthcare workers around the world

00:17:42.032 --> 00:17:44.352
but there's so many fields that I think

00:17:44.352 --> 00:17:45.809
between the edXs of the world

00:17:45.809 --> 00:17:47.082
and the Khan Academies of the world.

00:17:47.082 --> 00:17:48.312
I think we're actually just at the

00:17:48.312 --> 00:17:50.422
at the very early, early stages.

00:17:50.422 --> 00:17:52.605
I have another question
here and this is really,

00:17:52.605 --> 00:17:55.742
you're clearly an educator
reaching millions.

00:17:55.742 --> 00:17:57.262
So there's that but there's also

00:17:57.262 --> 00:18:00.682
you were used to be the
head of the AI lab at MIT.

00:18:00.682 --> 00:18:04.082
So you know a thing or two
I'm assuming about that.

00:18:04.082 --> 00:18:06.282
From YouTube (indistinct) asks

00:18:06.282 --> 00:18:07.722
what are your thoughts about the future

00:18:07.722 --> 00:18:10.022
of automation in different
fields of technology?

00:18:10.022 --> 00:18:11.524
And then I'll add to the question

00:18:11.524 --> 00:18:14.162
and given your views of where automation

00:18:14.162 --> 00:18:16.662
and machine learning
and robotics are going,

00:18:16.662 --> 00:18:18.722
where do you see education needing to go

00:18:18.722 --> 00:18:21.572
or maybe even leveraging
some of those trends?

00:18:21.572 --> 00:18:24.195
- Oh my God, there's so much
we could talk about here.

00:18:25.542 --> 00:18:29.952
Certainly the computer science
and AI lab at MIT is one

00:18:29.952 --> 00:18:33.012
of the centers for AI
machine learning research.

00:18:33.012 --> 00:18:34.600
A number of other universities and

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:38.012
and companies are also
where incredible research

00:18:38.012 --> 00:18:40.772
is happening in AI and machine learning.

00:18:40.772 --> 00:18:43.282
And much as computing

00:18:43.282 --> 00:18:45.522
and the internet changed the world

00:18:45.522 --> 00:18:50.142
in the late eighties,
machine learning and AI

00:18:50.142 --> 00:18:55.032
and automation are completely
transforming industry today.

00:18:55.032 --> 00:18:56.792
And in the future.

00:18:56.792 --> 00:18:59.302
The downside is that people talk

00:18:59.302 --> 00:19:03.142
about roughly 50% of today's
jobs will be transformed

00:19:03.142 --> 00:19:06.002
into other jobs that will require new sets

00:19:06.002 --> 00:19:08.794
of skills where the whole
planet, half the planet

00:19:08.794 --> 00:19:11.602
of people need to be
up-skilling and re-skilling.

00:19:11.602 --> 00:19:14.982
And that's one of the areas
that edX focuses in very heavily

00:19:14.982 --> 00:19:18.282
that we offer professional
certificates the micro masters

00:19:18.282 --> 00:19:20.602
and micro bachelors and other credentials

00:19:20.602 --> 00:19:22.942
in these topic areas
where people can come in

00:19:22.942 --> 00:19:24.842
and re-skill with short courses

00:19:24.842 --> 00:19:27.812
and programs and things like
machine learning and AI.

00:19:27.812 --> 00:19:31.612
But certainly the same
transformations of industries where

00:19:31.612 --> 00:19:34.502
in the past, I'd just
give you a simple example.

00:19:34.502 --> 00:19:37.665
Let's say for example, a translation,

00:19:38.932 --> 00:19:43.932
when you speak usually humans
would translate what you said

00:19:44.111 --> 00:19:46.722
into some other language
at the UN and so on.

00:19:46.722 --> 00:19:47.942
You've seen some of these,

00:19:47.942 --> 00:19:50.322
you've probably seen
the movie "Interpreter"

00:19:50.322 --> 00:19:53.915
where Nicole Kidman was out
there translating in real time

00:19:53.915 --> 00:19:55.372
what people were saying.

00:19:55.372 --> 00:19:57.852
Now, imagine today that this technology

00:19:57.852 --> 00:20:00.472
is there were even as you speak

00:20:00.472 --> 00:20:02.332
a machine could be translating you

00:20:02.332 --> 00:20:05.102
into one of any number of languages

00:20:05.102 --> 00:20:09.585
and machine learning technology
is key to things like this.

00:20:11.012 --> 00:20:13.263
Your second question, Sal.

00:20:13.263 --> 00:20:16.532
I think the same thing
huge impact will happen

00:20:16.532 --> 00:20:18.782
in the learning field as well

00:20:18.782 --> 00:20:21.512
where first of all, let me start by saying

00:20:21.512 --> 00:20:23.942
the teacher is always gonna be critical.

00:20:23.942 --> 00:20:27.212
I think people need to connect
with people and get inspired

00:20:27.212 --> 00:20:29.349
and they're looking for mentors and so on.

00:20:29.349 --> 00:20:33.102
And I too had some incredible
mentors in my school

00:20:33.102 --> 00:20:34.982
and in college.

00:20:34.982 --> 00:20:36.499
I'm hoping we can talk about some of that

00:20:36.499 --> 00:20:38.942
but at the same time machine learning

00:20:38.942 --> 00:20:41.182
and AI can take over many of the tasks.

00:20:41.182 --> 00:20:43.502
So for example, I'll give you one example.

00:20:43.502 --> 00:20:44.562
One of the things we worked on

00:20:44.562 --> 00:20:47.802
at edX was a (indistinct) framework.

00:20:47.802 --> 00:20:51.362
Where imagine based on
the solutions that people

00:20:51.362 --> 00:20:52.862
were developing for problems,

00:20:52.862 --> 00:20:55.922
imagine that you could create
a hinting framework where

00:20:55.922 --> 00:20:58.232
if a student got stuck in a
problem, that Sal can give me

00:20:58.232 --> 00:21:01.082
a hint and the computer
gives them a hint based

00:21:01.082 --> 00:21:03.012
on all the solutions
they've seen in the past.

00:21:03.012 --> 00:21:05.832
And so there are so many
things you could do.

00:21:05.832 --> 00:21:08.452
Another example is some
amazing work that Berkeley did

00:21:08.452 --> 00:21:12.042
where they did research
using edX learner data

00:21:12.042 --> 00:21:15.832
where looking at the
progression of learner activity

00:21:15.832 --> 00:21:18.322
they could tell when a
learner was going to drop

00:21:18.322 --> 00:21:19.442
out of a course.

00:21:19.442 --> 00:21:22.182
And so using machine learning
and then based on that

00:21:22.182 --> 00:21:24.672
you could actually create
interventions and mentorships

00:21:24.672 --> 00:21:27.182
that happened just at the
right time to enable someone

00:21:27.182 --> 00:21:29.582
to get more motivated
and to stay the course.

00:21:29.582 --> 00:21:31.212
And these are just two examples.

00:21:31.212 --> 00:21:33.772
Georgia Tech has done and
IBM have done research

00:21:33.772 --> 00:21:36.892
in some edX courses where
the teaching assistants

00:21:36.892 --> 00:21:40.122
in the discussion forums,
the live teaching assistants

00:21:40.122 --> 00:21:42.922
have been experimentally
in some cases replaced

00:21:42.922 --> 00:21:46.172
by the AI technology where
some of the questions

00:21:46.172 --> 00:21:49.632
were answered again, using
machine learning as a basis

00:21:49.632 --> 00:21:53.662
for building up the
repertoire of questions

00:21:53.662 --> 00:21:56.082
on which to train the
machine learning model.

00:21:56.082 --> 00:21:59.952
So I can see many, many areas
where learning and teachers

00:21:59.952 --> 00:22:04.159
and universities and schools
can benefit usually from AI

00:22:04.159 --> 00:22:08.432
and machine learning, helping
learners do a lot better.

00:22:08.432 --> 00:22:10.222
Imagine they, to me, one of the big ones

00:22:10.222 --> 00:22:13.402
is personalized learning where imagine

00:22:13.402 --> 00:22:16.732
if every learner could
have a personalized tutor

00:22:16.732 --> 00:22:20.099
that could teach them
problems in certain areas

00:22:20.099 --> 00:22:21.972
at their own pace

00:22:21.972 --> 00:22:24.132
in a way that was completely
customized to them.

00:22:24.132 --> 00:22:25.582
To me, that's one of the big holy grails

00:22:25.582 --> 00:22:27.585
of teaching and learning.

00:22:29.192 --> 00:22:31.932
- No, I couldn't agree more.

00:22:31.932 --> 00:22:36.262
These notions of personalization
but I am curious,

00:22:36.262 --> 00:22:37.902
how do you think this is going to affect

00:22:37.902 --> 00:22:40.542
and I agree with you also
that this will never replace

00:22:40.542 --> 00:22:42.782
the teacher, that human
connection actually

00:22:42.782 --> 00:22:44.603
was probably going to become even

00:22:44.603 --> 00:22:47.172
more important over time as
other things get automated.

00:22:47.172 --> 00:22:49.482
But what do you think this is
going to do to labor markets?

00:22:49.482 --> 00:22:51.022
Are there going to be,

00:22:51.022 --> 00:22:52.549
there's kind of these
techno optimists that say,

00:22:52.549 --> 00:22:53.972
"Well, the industrial revolution, yes

00:22:53.972 --> 00:22:56.802
a lot of jobs were lost but
a lot of jobs got created."

00:22:56.802 --> 00:22:58.402
Do you think that's just
going to be the same thing?

00:22:58.402 --> 00:23:01.632
We just have to have some
optimistic faith that will happen

00:23:01.632 --> 00:23:03.182
or do we think this time is different

00:23:03.182 --> 00:23:05.572
because of the nature of the
tasks that machine learning

00:23:05.572 --> 00:23:07.672
and robotics can take on.

00:23:07.672 --> 00:23:09.492
And if so, yeah,

00:23:09.492 --> 00:23:13.272
how do we get the positives
while mitigating the negatives?

00:23:13.272 --> 00:23:14.105
- I think we're seeing some

00:23:14.105 --> 00:23:16.918
of this happen today
where jobs get transformed

00:23:16.918 --> 00:23:21.918
into new jobs, where just imagine
that let's say for example

00:23:24.152 --> 00:23:28.452
you're driving a truck
and autonomous vehicles,

00:23:28.452 --> 00:23:32.132
self-driving cars are almost there,

00:23:32.132 --> 00:23:34.282
I think there's still some
tweaking that needs to be done

00:23:34.282 --> 00:23:35.482
but they're almost there.

00:23:35.482 --> 00:23:38.112
And so one could imagine that
you'll think, oh, my goodness

00:23:38.112 --> 00:23:40.942
we'll never have people
driving cars anymore.

00:23:40.942 --> 00:23:42.392
And for trucks that rumble

00:23:42.392 --> 00:23:43.952
from the west coast, the east coast

00:23:43.952 --> 00:23:46.792
or wherever in the world,
now you won't have drivers.

00:23:46.792 --> 00:23:50.842
But just imagine those would
be, you need other skills.

00:23:50.842 --> 00:23:53.342
You need people that maybe
you need a, I don't know

00:23:53.342 --> 00:23:55.989
maybe an IT technician or an IT expert

00:23:55.989 --> 00:24:00.662
in the truck and making
sure that all the IT

00:24:00.662 --> 00:24:02.022
and support systems are working well.

00:24:02.022 --> 00:24:04.342
Making sure that there
are fail safe models

00:24:04.342 --> 00:24:09.252
and making sure that when you
come to areas where the AI

00:24:09.252 --> 00:24:10.884
or machine learning doesn't quite cut it.

00:24:10.884 --> 00:24:14.072
I can imagine that if you're trying to,

00:24:14.072 --> 00:24:17.432
I've tried to drive a Vespa scooter

00:24:17.432 --> 00:24:20.952
on the small streets of
Muradabad near Delhi in India.

00:24:20.952 --> 00:24:23.206
And I promise you, I promise you

00:24:23.206 --> 00:24:25.682
I don't think that you can have

00:24:25.682 --> 00:24:28.512
any machine learning technology
do a self-driving car

00:24:28.512 --> 00:24:30.512
or something on the streets of Muradabad

00:24:30.512 --> 00:24:33.132
where any random molecule or a particle

00:24:33.132 --> 00:24:34.502
or biological entities coming

00:24:34.502 --> 00:24:37.362
from every which direction, it's insane.

00:24:37.362 --> 00:24:39.882
And so I think you need people for that.

00:24:39.882 --> 00:24:42.482
And, but I think we need more
people who are generalists

00:24:42.482 --> 00:24:45.062
that can do driving as
well as some other stuff.

00:24:45.062 --> 00:24:48.822
So I do see that jobs will
transform into newer jobs

00:24:48.822 --> 00:24:50.362
which is why this whole concept

00:24:50.362 --> 00:24:52.642
of up-skilling and
re-skilling is so important.

00:24:52.642 --> 00:24:54.402
In marketing a lot of the kind

00:24:54.402 --> 00:24:56.852
of marketing we did
earlier has now transformed

00:24:56.852 --> 00:25:00.212
to digital marketing,
analytics has taken off.

00:25:00.212 --> 00:25:05.102
And so today, data science
teams of companies have grown.

00:25:05.102 --> 00:25:08.312
They do machine learning,
data science, statistics

00:25:08.312 --> 00:25:10.722
and so on and those teams
are becoming bigger.

00:25:10.722 --> 00:25:13.712
So even as certain kinds
of teams are diminishing,

00:25:13.712 --> 00:25:16.222
you see the burgeoning of IT teams,

00:25:16.222 --> 00:25:18.302
you see burgeoning of data science teams.

00:25:18.302 --> 00:25:21.052
And I think the same kind
of trend we'll keep up,

00:25:21.052 --> 00:25:22.168
will keep happening.

00:25:22.168 --> 00:25:24.672
I mean look at the computing
when data centers went away

00:25:24.672 --> 00:25:26.401
to the cloud, people said, "Oh my God,

00:25:26.401 --> 00:25:30.202
so many jobs will be lost" but guess what?

00:25:30.202 --> 00:25:31.782
There's more jobs in computer science,

00:25:31.782 --> 00:25:33.822
more jobs in data centers.

00:25:33.822 --> 00:25:34.942
We need the ops people.

00:25:34.942 --> 00:25:36.892
We need ops support
people that understand how

00:25:36.892 --> 00:25:38.012
to do load balancing

00:25:38.012 --> 00:25:40.892
and security and privacy
and all of that stuff.

00:25:40.892 --> 00:25:44.852
So really jobs get transformed
and you need new kinds

00:25:44.852 --> 00:25:46.712
of skills or adjacent skills

00:25:46.712 --> 00:25:49.575
that people have to
really become expert on.

00:25:50.772 --> 00:25:52.602
- It's interesting, one of
the examples that was given

00:25:52.602 --> 00:25:54.812
to me recently which made a
lot of sense was something

00:25:54.812 --> 00:25:56.182
like online commerce.

00:25:56.182 --> 00:25:58.152
We imagine, I mean, it has

00:25:58.152 --> 00:26:00.412
in some ways disrupted a
lot of place-based retail

00:26:00.412 --> 00:26:03.672
but it didn't remove, although
maybe machine learning

00:26:03.672 --> 00:26:06.626
and robotics might change
some of it but still someone

00:26:06.626 --> 00:26:08.712
is picking things,
putting them in a basket.

00:26:08.712 --> 00:26:11.282
And actually it has created the new job

00:26:11.282 --> 00:26:13.822
of bringing it that last
mile to someone's door

00:26:13.822 --> 00:26:15.132
which wasn't there before

00:26:15.132 --> 00:26:16.452
because no one had the expectation

00:26:16.452 --> 00:26:18.952
that they would be able to
click a few things on an app

00:26:18.952 --> 00:26:21.302
and then their groceries
show up at their door

00:26:21.302 --> 00:26:23.858
or the book they wanna
buy shows up at the door

00:26:23.858 --> 00:26:26.262
within 24 hours.

00:26:26.262 --> 00:26:28.342
But it is gonna be interesting.

00:26:28.342 --> 00:26:31.142
I hope that we create more jobs

00:26:31.142 --> 00:26:32.967
and actually the jobs
are even higher value

00:26:32.967 --> 00:26:35.192
but there is this notion of technology

00:26:35.192 --> 00:26:37.072
is hyper-productive which means

00:26:37.072 --> 00:26:39.684
that you can get the same
output with less labor.

00:26:39.684 --> 00:26:44.082
So but my gut sense is
we, edX and Khan Academy,

00:26:44.082 --> 00:26:45.722
we got a role to play to make sure

00:26:45.722 --> 00:26:47.802
that most of the jobs are gonna be higher

00:26:47.802 --> 00:26:51.682
up the value chain, more kind
of knowledge economy jobs.

00:26:51.682 --> 00:26:54.322
And so we need to make sure
more people are prepared

00:26:54.322 --> 00:26:57.558
to be there in this world we're going to.

00:26:57.558 --> 00:27:00.162
(crosstalk)

00:27:00.162 --> 00:27:02.162
- I think what's interesting here Sal is

00:27:03.123 --> 00:27:04.772
that I actually see this maybe goes back

00:27:04.772 --> 00:27:05.782
to quantum mechanics.

00:27:05.782 --> 00:27:08.772
I see sort of a cyclical
thing happening here

00:27:08.772 --> 00:27:11.032
where as we think we are climbing

00:27:11.032 --> 00:27:14.082
up the ladder and learning the
newer and higher tech skills

00:27:14.082 --> 00:27:16.992
what we're also seeing
within enterprises is

00:27:16.992 --> 00:27:19.538
that more and more employees
and companies are doubling

00:27:19.538 --> 00:27:22.912
down on what they call soft skills.

00:27:22.912 --> 00:27:24.852
Really, it's better to
call them power skills

00:27:24.852 --> 00:27:28.632
or human skills where empathy and teamwork

00:27:28.632 --> 00:27:33.142
and collaboration, creativity,
design thinking, empathy.

00:27:33.142 --> 00:27:35.352
These are all becoming
a lot more important

00:27:35.352 --> 00:27:37.299
where machines don't quite do that.

00:27:37.299 --> 00:27:40.572
And so I think we will see
and if we're going back

00:27:40.572 --> 00:27:45.112
to the future approach where
or maybe more like future

00:27:45.112 --> 00:27:46.102
to the past or whatever

00:27:46.102 --> 00:27:48.522
where the human skills will be emphasized

00:27:48.522 --> 00:27:51.342
where whether in companies
or in teaching and so on

00:27:51.342 --> 00:27:54.742
the human skills will be
emphasized where people can do

00:27:54.742 --> 00:27:56.842
what they're good at which is being human.

00:27:58.502 --> 00:28:01.202
- I couldn't agree more.

00:28:01.202 --> 00:28:03.091
I think there's this
something about technology

00:28:03.091 --> 00:28:05.882
and as we get some of
our material needs met

00:28:05.882 --> 00:28:08.122
by more and more productive technology,

00:28:08.122 --> 00:28:10.802
we realize that some of these
core ideas that we've had

00:28:10.802 --> 00:28:13.082
for hundreds of thousands
of years of human history.

00:28:13.082 --> 00:28:15.982
The community being part of a
village, being part of a clan,

00:28:15.982 --> 00:28:18.422
being part of a tribe, that we've gone

00:28:18.422 --> 00:28:21.212
through a first phase of
technology kind of separating us.

00:28:21.212 --> 00:28:24.972
And we see it, anxiety,
depression, loneliness

00:28:24.972 --> 00:28:27.762
are our really epidemics and
disproportionately epidemic.

00:28:27.762 --> 00:28:32.482
It seems in the Western more
materially successful world.

00:28:32.482 --> 00:28:34.342
And I hope you're right, that as we,

00:28:34.342 --> 00:28:37.112
as more things get automated,
we start realizing, hey

00:28:37.112 --> 00:28:39.682
that frees us up to kind of be the humans

00:28:39.682 --> 00:28:41.912
in the village and connect
with each other emotionally

00:28:41.912 --> 00:28:43.962
and people talk about healthcare.

00:28:43.962 --> 00:28:45.212
There's probably gonna be a lot of jobs

00:28:45.212 --> 00:28:46.842
it's just make sure
people have companionship,

00:28:46.842 --> 00:28:50.012
make sure, there's a lot of
studies that the best doctors

00:28:50.012 --> 00:28:51.820
who cares what the
medicine they're giving is,

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:54.982
does the patient feel
like they're being heard

00:28:54.982 --> 00:28:56.712
or they feel like they're being cared for?

00:28:56.712 --> 00:28:59.852
So and this goes go back
to the quantum physics

00:28:59.852 --> 00:29:04.852
and Nisargadatta Maharaj around
perception is often reality.

00:29:04.882 --> 00:29:06.222
And this feeling of connectedness.

00:29:06.222 --> 00:29:09.400
A feeling is a real thing
that we can't undertake.

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:10.682
- Exactly, exactly.

00:29:10.682 --> 00:29:13.682
- Well, this got way more
philosophical than I had expected.

00:29:13.682 --> 00:29:14.892
I thought we were just gonna talk

00:29:14.892 --> 00:29:16.452
about democratizing education

00:29:16.452 --> 00:29:18.602
which is a big idea in of itself.

00:29:18.602 --> 00:29:20.622
But you have a lot of folks, young people,

00:29:20.622 --> 00:29:22.622
people of all ages, I mean

00:29:22.622 --> 00:29:27.622
I'll let you take us home
on where is all this going?

00:29:28.092 --> 00:29:29.412
Where do you hope this goes?

00:29:29.412 --> 00:29:32.435
What advice, what should
be people's take aways?

00:29:33.782 --> 00:29:35.542
- I think it's very simple.

00:29:35.542 --> 00:29:38.142
Khan Academy, edX and so on,

00:29:38.142 --> 00:29:39.632
have been doing online learning.

00:29:39.632 --> 00:29:42.112
You've been doing it
for, oh my God, 15 years.

00:29:42.112 --> 00:29:44.895
We've been doing it at
edX for about 10 years.

00:29:46.002 --> 00:29:48.842
And we began doing this
well before the pandemic

00:29:48.842 --> 00:29:50.852
but then the pandemic hit about a year ago

00:29:50.852 --> 00:29:52.302
and the whole world went

00:29:52.302 --> 00:29:56.025
to online learning and
online working, remote work.

00:29:57.112 --> 00:29:59.302
I think this is here to stay.

00:29:59.302 --> 00:30:01.072
I think the one message
I have for everybody is

00:30:01.072 --> 00:30:03.892
that the future is gonna be
blended where whether you're

00:30:03.892 --> 00:30:08.822
in school or college, there's
gonna be a combination

00:30:08.822 --> 00:30:11.302
of online learning and in-person learning.

00:30:11.302 --> 00:30:14.392
We will figure it out what are
the best things to do online?

00:30:14.392 --> 00:30:15.842
What are the best things to do in person?

00:30:15.842 --> 00:30:17.642
So for example, lectures

00:30:17.642 --> 00:30:19.992
isn't it better to sit in front of a,

00:30:19.992 --> 00:30:22.572
watch short videos and I
call them Khan-style videos,

00:30:22.572 --> 00:30:24.762
five, 10 minute videos that you can pause,

00:30:24.762 --> 00:30:26.142
rewind as many times as you want.

00:30:26.142 --> 00:30:27.502
And you can do it at midnight.

00:30:27.502 --> 00:30:28.652
Isn't that so much better than going

00:30:28.652 --> 00:30:30.732
to a lecture for 60 minutes

00:30:30.732 --> 00:30:33.452
or worst 90 minutes at eight
o'clock in the morning?

00:30:33.452 --> 00:30:35.172
And those things are better than online.

00:30:35.172 --> 00:30:38.172
However, some other things
like maybe collaborating

00:30:38.172 --> 00:30:42.732
with a couple of dorm
mates on a problem set

00:30:42.732 --> 00:30:44.712
or building something

00:30:44.712 --> 00:30:47.992
or working one-on-one with a professor,

00:30:47.992 --> 00:30:49.499
some of these things might
be better than online.

00:30:49.499 --> 00:30:51.912
And I think the figured
out the blended model.

00:30:51.912 --> 00:30:53.564
So to me, my last message is that

00:30:53.564 --> 00:30:58.562
when you're in school, I
would focus on making sure

00:30:58.562 --> 00:31:01.382
that you learn how to
learn, not just in person

00:31:01.382 --> 00:31:03.372
but also learn how to learn online.

00:31:03.372 --> 00:31:04.962
And we actually launched a couple

00:31:04.962 --> 00:31:06.942
of courses on edX on how to learn online.

00:31:06.942 --> 00:31:09.415
This would be a very critical
skill going in the end

00:31:09.415 --> 00:31:11.422
because in the future, all
learning will be a combination

00:31:11.422 --> 00:31:13.455
of online and in-person learning.

00:31:15.042 --> 00:31:16.772
- I love it, I couldn't agree more.

00:31:16.772 --> 00:31:21.362
We always have a good time
chatting Professor Agarwal.

00:31:21.362 --> 00:31:22.852
I thank you so much for joining us.

00:31:22.852 --> 00:31:25.082
And I'm excited to go
on this journey with you

00:31:25.082 --> 00:31:29.495
'cause I think it could make
a big dent in the universe.

00:31:30.362 --> 00:31:31.332
- Well, fantastic, Sal.

00:31:31.332 --> 00:31:32.412
Always great chatting with you.

00:31:32.412 --> 00:31:33.572
You've been an inspiration.

00:31:33.572 --> 00:31:35.921
I would not have been doing this

00:31:35.921 --> 00:31:38.602
had it not been for the work that you did.

00:31:38.602 --> 00:31:40.206
And so just keep up all that you do

00:31:40.206 --> 00:31:42.592
and hope we continue to collaborate

00:31:42.592 --> 00:31:44.292
and do great things in the future.

00:31:45.432 --> 00:31:46.980
- Thank you so much.

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:48.130
- [Anant] Thank you.

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:50.252
Well, thanks everyone for joining.

00:31:50.252 --> 00:31:52.032
As you could tell very fun conversation

00:31:52.032 --> 00:31:56.152
and myself and Anant go
way, way, way back probably

00:31:56.152 --> 00:31:57.312
before he remembers it.

00:31:57.312 --> 00:32:00.182
But he was, I don't
remember all my professors

00:32:00.182 --> 00:32:01.722
but I definitely remember him.

00:32:01.722 --> 00:32:05.682
I had no idea that our lives
would converge in this way

00:32:05.682 --> 00:32:08.672
but every few months we get
together when one of us is in,

00:32:08.672 --> 00:32:10.852
when he's either in the
Bay area or I'm in Boston.

00:32:10.852 --> 00:32:13.872
And we ended up spending hours chatting

00:32:13.872 --> 00:32:15.652
about a million different things on

00:32:15.652 --> 00:32:16.832
a million different dimension.

00:32:16.832 --> 00:32:19.842
So a really, really
interesting guy and edX I think

00:32:19.842 --> 00:32:23.852
is doing some incredibly
powerful work and it really picks

00:32:23.852 --> 00:32:26.912
up where to some degree
Khan Academy leaves off.

00:32:26.912 --> 00:32:30.422
And so I think between
these types of organizations

00:32:30.422 --> 00:32:32.133
and others, there's a real chance

00:32:32.133 --> 00:32:34.162
to provide really rich resources

00:32:34.162 --> 00:32:37.322
for learners and for
educators around the world.

00:32:37.322 --> 00:32:39.202
But thank you so much for joining.

00:32:39.202 --> 00:32:40.102
Thank you for being (indistinct),

00:32:40.102 --> 00:32:41.102
I'm sorry for all of y'all.

00:32:41.102 --> 00:32:42.682
I couldn't get to all of your questions.

